ImmuLog — Split Licensing
==========================

ImmuLog is licensed under two licenses, split by component:

  Component                       License
  ------------------------------  ----------------------------------
  core/                           Apache License 2.0
  internal/web/                   GNU AGPL v3.0 or later
  main.go                         GNU AGPL v3.0 or later
  web/                            GNU AGPL v3.0 or later
  everything else                 GNU AGPL v3.0 or later

Full texts:

  LICENSES/Apache-2.0.txt
  LICENSES/AGPL-3.0-or-later.txt

Each source file carries an `SPDX-License-Identifier` tag; when this file
and a file header disagree, the file header wins.


Why the split
-------------

`core/` (package `gitx` + package `feed`) turns a plain Git repository into a
tamper-evident, append-only message log: content-addressed messages, signed
identities, local witness anchors, key-rotation chains. It is a general-purpose
library with no server, no HTTP and no third-party dependencies.

It is Apache-2.0 so that **anyone can build on it without asking** — including
building a completely different client, or embedding it in a product that never
touches our server.

Everything else is the running service: the HTTP/SSE transport and the
integrity/sync loops. It is AGPL-3.0-or-later so that **anyone who runs this
code as a network service must keep their version of it open**. Improving
ImmuLog and offering it to others as a closed service is not permitted.


One fact that must not be glossed over
--------------------------------------

The default build produces a **single binary** that links both halves. Because
Apache-2.0 is compatible with AGPL-3.0, that combination is allowed — but
**distributing that binary means distributing an AGPL-3.0 work**, and all of
AGPL-3.0's obligations apply to it.

So the Apache grant on `core/` is meaningful for exactly one thing, which is the
thing that matters: **a third party can `go get` the core and reuse it under
permissive terms, without ever touching our service.** That is why `core/` is
not under `internal/` — Go forbids importing `internal/`, which would have made
the Apache grant decorative.


Contributions
-------------

Contributions are accepted under the Developer Certificate of Origin (see
`DCO`), not a CLA. That means **you keep your copyright**, and the project
cannot be relicensed later without the consent of every contributor.

See `CONTRIBUTING.md`.


